<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- selectableAsDefault="false" is the whole point of this file, and leaving
     it out was a real bug with the blast radius of the entire phone.

     AOSP defaults this attribute to TRUE (RecognitionServiceInfo: "boolean
     selectableAsDefault = true; // default"). With that default, and with
     the android.speech.RecognitionService intent-filter this service used to
     declare, the system enumerated JarvisRecognitionServiceStub as a
     candidate system speech recogniser and offered it in Settings -> Voice
     input. Had it ever been picked - by hand, or by findAvailRecognizer()
     falling through to the first available service when the configured one
     went missing - then EVERY SpeechRecognizer call on the device would have
     returned ERROR_CLIENT instantly, because that is all this stub does.
     Gboard voice typing, every other app, with nothing naming Jarvis as the
     cause. From an app whose own rule is that it does no speech recognition.

     The intent-filter is gone for the same reason and costs nothing:
     VoiceInteractionServiceInfo reads the `recognitionService` ATTRIBUTE
     STRING out of voice_interaction_service.xml and resolves the component
     directly. It never queries by action, so the filter bought nothing and
     was the thing that made this discoverable.

     No settingsActivity: there is nothing to configure on a service that
     never recognises anything - see JarvisRecognitionServiceStub's own doc. -->
<recognition-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:selectableAsDefault="false" />
